[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Candidate shape: a flat rule list with a per-site action, Kubernetes-admission style

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-CANDIDATE-ADMISSION-RULES
TITLE: Candidate shape: a flat rule list with a per-site action, Kubernetes-admission style
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
PROPOSED SHAPE, one of three, from the 2026-08-29 prior-art spike
(SPIKE-DSL-PRIOR-ART). Not chosen. All three show the SAME worked example so
they compare by eye: a DECISION whose STATUS moves from open to accepted only
when a guard holds, a dirty-edge rule over one relation role, one gate CI can
call, and one actor primitive keyed on an ssh identity.

WHAT IT BORROWS. Kubernetes ValidatingAdmissionPolicy: a flat list of rules, each with a match, an expression, and a per-binding action (Deny | Warn | Audit). No lifecycle object at all; a lifecycle is just rules on STATUS changes, and the site is explicit on every rule.

THE SHAPE.

  { c, ... }: {
    canon.rules.decision-accept = {
      match = { nodeType = "DECISION"; field = "STATUS"; to = "accepted"; };
      when = c.and [
        (c.eq (c.var "before.STATUS") "open")                # the "from" state is just a clause
        (c.eq (c.var "actor.class") "human")
        (c.every "Governed_By"
          (c.inList (c.var "target.DEPTH") [ "interface-settled" "implemented" "verified" ]))
      ];
      actions."scribe.change" = "deny";                      # deny | warn | audit, per joint
      message = "every governing node must be at least interface-settled";
    };
    canon.rules.no-stale-edges = {
      match = { relation = "Governed_By"; };
      when = c.eq (c.var "edge.stale") false;
      actions = { "git.commit" = "warn"; check = "deny"; };  # legal in the canon, fatal at check
    };
    canon.ripple.Governed_By = { onFields = [ "STATEMENT" ]; clearedBy = [ "human" ]; };
    canon.actors = {
      extractor = "ssh-signing-key";
      classes.human = [ "SHA256:AbCd..." ];
      default = "agent";
    };
    # CI runs:  scribe check     (every rule whose check action is "deny")
  }

HOW IT DECOMPOSES ONTO THE HOOK LAYER (MECH-HOOK-MIDDLEWARE-INTERFACE).
Each rule -> one hook per key in actions: deny -> PRE handler (stop), warn/audit -> POST handler that records; the check key -> membership in `scribe check`.
match -> the hook's filter (node type, field, to) evaluated by the generic evaluator before when.
ripple and actors -> as in the other candidates; there is nothing else to compile.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
STRENGTHS.
- One construct, and each rule is literally one hook; the DSL-to-L2 mapping is a table, not a compiler.
- Site tiering the operator asked for (dirty allowed, warn at commit, deny at merge) is written on the rule itself, in the runtime's own words.
- Smallest option surface to type and document; the flake check for it is a few assertions.

WEAKNESSES.
- No lifecycle object, so Nix cannot check reachability or 'no two transitions on one trigger'; the from state is a clause Nix cannot see through.
- Nothing to draw: the state graph exists only in the reader's head, which defeats 'easy to read and reason about'.
- Augment is fine (add a rule) but overshadow is coarse: mkForce replaces a whole rule, and two rules on the same match compose by AND with no way to say 'instead'.

Recorded from the spike's synthesis without endorsement; the operator weighs the three by reading the shapes.
<<<
